% ===========================================================================
%  references.bib -- only entries whose metadata was verified against the
%  publisher, the proceedings, or the project's own pages. Nothing here is
%  reconstructed from memory.
%
%  Verified on 2026-11-02:
%    - COLM 2024 accepted-papers list + Microsoft Research publication page
%      (AutoGen: title, 13 authors, venue).
%    - NeurIPS proceedings pages (Zheng et al. 2023; Panickssery et al. 2024).
%    - IJCAI 1973 proceedings (Hewitt, Bishop, Steiger; pp. 235--245).
%    - Pragmatic Bookshelf product page (Nygard, 2nd ed., ISBN 9781680502398).
%    - Project pages for LangChain, LangGraph, CrewAI (no formal venue exists
%      for a software package, so these are misc entries with howpublished).
%
%  WITNESS NOTE. Three entries below carry an eprint field because a
%  registration agency can re-resolve them: AutoGen 2308.08155, MT-Bench
%  2306.05685, and Panickssery 2404.13076, each confirmed against
%  export.arxiv.org by the sidecar's CitationAuditProxy.
%
%  Those same three also repeat the identifier as a clickable note. This is not
%  redundancy for its own sake: IEEEtran.bst has no eprint handler, so the
%  machine-readable field is silently dropped at typesetting time. A witness
%  that exists only in the .bib is a witness for the auditor, not for the
%  reader -- and a reader who cannot resolve the identifier has to take the
%  citation on faith, which is exactly what this paper argues against. The
%  eprint field serves tooling; the note serves people; neither substitutes for
%  the other.
%
%  The other eight split two ways as of the Zenodo v1 deposit. Three now carry
%  a DataCite DOI (the two software entries and the method-transfer manuscript,
%  see the DOI POLICY note below): resolvable at doi.org, but invisible to a
%  CrossRef-only resolver, so they audit as INVALID under this project's own
%  proxy while being perfectly verifiable by hand. The remaining five have no
%  identifier at all -- not false, but unverifiable by anyone who does not
%  already trust this file. That is the paper's own conclusion applied to the
%  paper's own bibliography, and it is recorded rather than papered over. Do not
%  invent an identifier to turn an INVALID green: a plausible-looking id is
%  exactly the failure this file is meant to survive.
%
%  NOTE ON THIS FILE'S SYNTAX. BibTeX has no comment syntax inside an entry:
%  a percent sign there is data, not a comment, and it will abort the file.
%  Likewise a stray at-sign anywhere outside an entry -- including inside a
%  comment line -- starts a new entry. Keep every remark in these top-of-file
%  comments, and never write an at-sign in one.
%
%  NOTE ON THE STYLE. This document sets its bibliography with IEEEtran.bst
%  1.14, which has two relevant limits. First, it defines no ISBN handler:
%  an isbn field is read and silently dropped, so the Nygard entry repeats its
%  ISBN inside note (which is printed after the publisher). Keep the two in
%  sync -- the field is for machines, the note is for readers. Second, its
%  format.note lowercases the FIRST character of a note unless the note begins
%  with a brace; that is an escape hatch written into the style itself. Hence
%  the doubled braces in every note below: with a single pair, "ISBN ..."
%  would print as "iSBN ...".
%
%  The two software entries below use the biblatex entry type for software
%  (entry type name: software), which is not a BibTeX type.
%  BibTeX routes an undefined type to the style's default.type (here: misc),
%  so they typeset correctly but emit one "entry type isn't style-file
%  defined" warning each run. That is expected noise, not an error.
% ===========================================================================

